-- Story 7.4 (UC-SCH5-001 sub-pages) seed: the itemized Other Camp (62) / Other Access (68) expense
-- rows and the contexts that exercise them. TEST-SCOPE ONLY. Seeds into the THE.CAMP_REPORT table
-- and the CAMP_REPORT_ID FK column that V34 created; never edits either.
--
-- ================================================================================================
-- TIMESTAMP VERSION, per db/README.md:26-28 and the V20260807 precedent. The integer convention has
-- now collided four times (schedules 2, 11, 6, and V34's near-miss); 20260812 orders after
-- 20260807, so this applies immediately after the Story 7.2 fixtures it sits beside, and no future
-- merge can force a renumber of this file or of anyone else's.
-- ================================================================================================
--
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SNAPSHOT WIDENING (Task 1 gate (i)) -- the one DDL statement here, and it is a FIDELITY fix.
--
-- Delivery declares ITEM_DESCRIPTION as VARCHAR2(120) with CHAR_USED = 'B' (byte semantics on an
-- AL32UTF8 database). The V1 snapshot declares VARCHAR2(30), which is BYTE semantics too by Oracle's
-- default. That gap makes a green IT prove less than it appears to: a 30-character description
-- containing any multi-byte character is ~90 bytes, which delivery accepts and a 30-BYTE column
-- rejects with ORA-12899 -- so the local suite would fail on input production accepts, or (worse,
-- once someone "fixes" it by using ASCII everywhere) silently stop covering the case.
--
-- Widening to match delivery also makes one assertion honest: @MaxByteLength(120) behind
-- @Size(max = 30) can NEVER fire, because a 30-CHARACTER string carries at most 90 UTF-8 bytes
-- (thirty 3-byte BMP characters; a 4-byte character is a surrogate pair and costs two of the thirty,
-- so it is less byte-dense, not more). Schedule5SubPageValidationIT therefore asserts that a
-- 30-character multibyte description is ACCEPTED, which is only meaningful against a column wider
-- than 30 bytes -- i.e. only after this widening.
--
-- Safe by construction: these migrations run against a throwaway Testcontainer only and are never
-- applied to the shared dev/prod THE schema (db/README.md:3-7, AD-2 -- no runtime DDL).
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALTER TABLE THE.ILCR_COST_REPORT_DETAIL MODIFY (ITEM_DESCRIPTION VARCHAR2(120));

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ID ranges. A NEW block, claimed after scanning every seed migration on disk: the highest
-- ILCR_COST_REPORT_DETAIL_ID in use anywhere is 8438 (V34's read fixtures). This story takes
--   CAMP_REPORT_ID              8700-8719
--   ILCR_COST_REPORT_DETAIL_ID  8720-8799
-- both clear of Schedule 5 write's 8200-8229/8230-8299, Schedule 6's 8301-8399, Schedule 5 read's
-- 8401-8410/8411-8438 and Schedule 8's >= 8500, and both BELOW the sequence starts
-- (ILCR_REPORT_COMMON_SEQ 9500, ILCR_COST_REPORT_DETAIL_SEQ 9000) so ids the app draws at runtime
-- cannot collide with a fixture. Mills 690-693 are likewise new: seeded mill ids stop at 676 and do
-- not resume until 1001. Registered in db/README.md.
--
-- Isolation model (the V21/V32 lesson, restated by V20260807): a context is (mill, YEAR), so every
-- DESTRUCTIVE concern claims its own year on mill 690 and NEVER reuses Story 7.2's mills 670-676.
-- Tests that only assert rejections mutate nothing and may share a year -- their per-row fingerprint
-- is what proves nothing persisted.
--
-- No cost item is inserted: items 62, 68, 141 and 142 are all already registered by V34/V31, and
-- cost-item ids are shared master data (db/README.md:66-68).
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- ================================================================================================
-- Mill 690 -- the sub-page write playground (Schedule5SubPageIT). ACT + Draft 2016-2023:
--   2016  camp 8700, three item-62 rows + two item-68 rows -> the reconcile round trip
--   2017  camp 8701, two item-62 rows                      -> immediate row DELETE
--   2018  camp 8702, no rows                               -> insert-from-empty, and empty-clears
--   2019  camp 8703, one item-62 row with EVERY cost NULL  -> 7.1 deviation (h)/(L): camp side
--                                                             serves 0, access side serves null
--   2020  camp 8704 + camp 8705                            -> the foreign-row 404 pair (a row of
--                                                             8705 offered to 8704)
--   2021  camp 8706, one item-62 + one item-68 row         -> the cross-ITEM 404 (deviation (O)):
--                                                             an item-68 row id offered to the camp
--                                                             page must 404, not cross-write
--   2022  camp 8707, backdated ENTRY_* (2020-01-01)        -> the ENTRY_*-unchanged-on-UPDATE proof.
--                                                             Backdated deliberately: the audit
--                                                             columns are DATE (gate (i)), so two
--                                                             writes inside one second are
--                                                             indistinguishable and a same-day
--                                                             baseline could not falsify a re-stamp.
--   2023  camp 8708, one item-62 row cost 9999999          -> rejection probes only; mutates nothing
--   2028  camp 8718, one item-62 row with desc AND cost    -> the UPDATE-to-NULL proof (a cleared
--                                                             field is cleared, not kept)
-- ================================================================================================
